[Position Overview]
You are not an ordinary "test driver," but a "city decoder" and "version evolution witness" of the assisted driving system. Your core mission is: 
Driving the latest version of assisted driving vehicles daily, it "actively finds faults" on real city roads, uncovers extreme scene flaws, compares performance across different versions, and ultimately becomes the city's most knowledgeable system performance and terrain pain points — a "living map" and "scenario expert" 
。 
Your work will directly influence the direction of product iteration—your feedback is the "ammunition" for engineers fixing bugs, the "compass" for product managers to optimize experience, and the "yardstick" for security teams to assess risk. 
[Core Responsibilities]
Daily generalization tests are performed
Drive test vehicles equipped with the latest version of the assisted driving system daily, conducting real road tests in different urban areas (commercial districts, elevated roads, tunnels, school districts, construction sections, urban-rural fringes, nighttime/rain/fog/snow, etc.); 
Focus on capturing the system's performance in "non-ideal scenarios": such as sudden lane changes, peeking pedestrians, unmarked intersections, temporary cones, blocking traffic lights, blurred lane markings, slow traffic in congestion, and sudden braking follow-up; 
Maintain "user perspective + engineering thinking," recording system behavior, user responses, security boundaries, and interaction experience. 
Version comparison testing experts
Compare performance differences among different software versions in the same scenario, including response speed, recognition accuracy, decision rationality, takeover frequency, and clarity of user prompts; 
Establish a "version comparison matrix" and output a "Version Iteration Performance Comparison Report," clearly identifying "which scenarios have improved," "which scenarios have deteriorated," and "which issues have recurred"; 
Provides key data support for pre-release "regression testing" and "risk assessment." 
Regional Scene Expert (City Living Map)
After long-term continuous testing, he became the city's "best understanding of system real-vehicle performance": clearly knowing which intersections the system easily misjudges, which sections tend to lose lanes, and which weather conditions perform worst; 
Establish a "generalized problem scenario database," marking high-risk road sections, high-frequency failure points, and typical user confusion points, forming a reusable "golden testing route"; 
Provides "localized testing guides" for new testers, engineers, and product managers, becoming the team's "scenario knowledge hub." 
Defect mining and closed-loop feedback
Every defect case is fully recorded during testing, producing a "reproducible, analyzable, and traceable" problem report; 
Participate in defect review meetings, work with algorithm teams to analyze root causes and promote problem closures; 
Perform "regression verification" on the fixed version to ensure the issue is truly resolved and does not recur. 
User perspective experience accumulation
It is recommended to optimize interaction prompts, voice reminders, and security redundancy mechanisms to enhance user security and system availability; 
Participated in designing user education content such as "Beginner Guide" and "Functional Boundary Explanation." 
[Job Requirements]
Required Requirements: 
Hold a Chinese C1 or above driver's license, with ≥ 2 years of driving experience, no major accidents or violations; 
Passionate about automotive technology, with a strong interest in intelligent driving systems, familiar with assisted driving functions of mainstream brands (Tesla, NIO, XPeng, Li Auto, Huawei ADS, Xiaomi, etc.); 
Having a "spot-the-difference" spirit: skilled at observing details, noticing anomalies, recording problems, and able to tolerate repeated tests, extreme weather, and congested road conditions; 
Excellent communication and documentation skills: able to clearly describe issues, write reports, and participate in cross-team discussions; 
Able to accept flexible working hours (morning and evening rush hours, nights, holidays, etc.) and adapt to citywide testing. 
Bonus points: 
Experience in automotive testing, autonomous driving testing, software testing, and user research; 
Familiar with test case design and defect management processes (such as JIRA and Zendao); 
Experience living in local cities (e.g., more than 3 years in Guangzhou/Shanghai), familiar with local road conditions, traffic rules, and peak hours; 
Capable of shooting vlogs or writing evaluations, and can assist in producing test case videos. 
[What We Offer]
Deeply participate in the iteration of cutting-edge intelligent driving systems; your feedback will directly drive product evolution; 
Collaborate with top algorithm, engineering, and product teams to become a dual expert in "system performance + local scenarios";

