Responsibilities
1. Job Background
Currently, the main factors affecting the capabilities of embodied manipulation and locomanipulation models are the extremely difficulty of obtaining high-quality data and the lack of benchmarks that can measure true capabilities. We are building simulation and evaluation systems for embodied and whole-body mobility operations: using physical simulation, World Model, and generative simulation to mass-produce data that "cannot be collected, cannot be collected, or cannot be fully collected"; At the same time, tasks, metrics, and regression protocols are made into reproducible benchmarks, making every improvement in operational strategies and locomanipulation strategies traceable and cross-methodological. 
2. Job Responsibilities
1. Design of simulation systems for data gaps
Built a unified simulation base for embodied operations (grabbing, placement, tool usage, contact force) and loco-manipulation (walking and operation, full-body loading, two-arm coordination). Covering multiple bodies, scenarios, objects, and long-tail perturbations, simulation outputs can be directly integrated into simulation learning / VLA / operation control strategy training sets, rather than just visualization. 
2. Generative simulation and World Model data engine
Using generative scenes/assets/trajectories, videos, or state World Models to fill in the distribution that real machines cannot cover: occlusion, slip, contact failures, load changes, terrain and whole-body coupling. Explore a hybrid data solution where "physical simulation provides constraints, generative models provide diversity," reducing the cost of trial and error for remote operation and real devices, and serving large-scale training of operational models and whole-body strategies. 
3. Effective benchmark construction
Define reproducible evaluation protocols for operations and loco-manipulation: beyond success rates, it also covers contact stability, operations while moving, generalization to new objects or terrains, and failure diagnostics. Construct standard task sets, baselines, anti-cheating, and daily regression to replace the "see the real demo" evaluation method, becoming model selection and access control. 
4. Iteration of evaluation-driven approaches
Establish feedback between simulation closed-loop, synthetic data, remote operation data, and real device cases: which tasks lack data, which metrics are insensitive, and which have the largest sim-to-real gaps. Use evaluation results to determine the priority for the next round of collection and generation, rather than just piling data on feel. 
5. Platformization and collaboration
Collaborating with algorithm, operations control, and data collection teams to provide large-scale parallel rollouts, data pipelines, and evaluation services, ensuring training reproducibility, fair comparison, and automatic regression execution, supporting continuous iteration of operational models and loco-manipulation strategies. 

Requirements
1. Master's degree or above in computer science, artificial intelligence, graphics, automation, robotics, or related fields; 
2. Understand data and evaluation pain points of embodied operations or loco-manipulation, and be familiar with at least one category: physical simulation, generative data synthesis, World Model, and robot evaluation systems; 
3. Familiar with at least one simulation stack such as Isaac / MuJoCo, able to build tasks, sensors, contacts, and domain randomization, and produce trajectory/video data usable for training; 
4. Proficient in Python and proficient in using PyTorch; Able to independently complete data pipelines, review scripts, and problem localization; 
