Responsibilities
1. Job Background
Motion control has begun evolving from blind movement to real-world perception and interaction, but the challenges are more difficult: maintaining balance while moving, completing operations through contact, and treating full-body degrees of freedom as a unified system in long-range tasks. 
We are building next-generation loco-manipulation systems for humanoid and mobile operation ontologies, connecting a complete chain from whole-body data collection and movement control to collaborative operation of upper and lower limbs. 
2. Job Responsibilities
1. Loco-manipulation architecture design
Exploring whole-body motor control, reinforcement learning/imitation learning, Diffusion Policy / Flow Matching, and the unification of VLA/VA/WAM in mobile operations. Breaking through the modular stitching of "lower limb tracking + independent upper limb grasping," the model shares balance and contact constraints among walking, squatting, turning, bearing, and arm operation, evolving toward a unified full-body loco-manipulation strategy. 
2. Closed-loop control and strategy for whole-body operations
Design trainable and deployable whole-body control and operation interfaces: state estimation, whole-body MPC / WBC / RL strategies, operation primitives and end-point force control, and integration with higher-level task strategies. Provide reproducible training and real-machine solutions for long-duration, sparse rewards, multi-terrain, and multi-task migration. 
3. Construction of a humanoid data collection system
Constructs a closed-loop loco-manipulation data covering remote operation, motion redirection, simulation augmentation, and real-device failure replay: full-body motion acquisition chain (motion capture / VR / exoskeleton / master-slave arm), timing alignment, contact and force annotation, trajectory slicing, and quality control. Each acquisition directly serves training, evaluation, and regression. 
4. Review and data flywheel
In-depth involvement in simulation and real device evaluation: balancing stability, landing success rate, grabbing/placing while moving, contact impact, and long-tail disturbance. Efficient feedback is established between simulation closed loops, remote operation data, and real machine failure cases, using data to drive the next round of priority for operation control and acquisition systems. 
5. Software-hardware collaboration from training to deployment
Achieve strategy distillation, quantization, control frequency, and communication scheduling optimization for real robots, collaborating with embedded/operations control teams to balance flexibility and safety under real latency, torque, power consumption, and protection constraints, and turning laboratory capabilities into loco-manipulation capabilities that can be stably demonstrated and iteratively mass-produced. 

